var tafsirData_46 = {
  "1": "“Ḥâ-Mĩm” opens the seventh and final chapter in the connected group beginning with these letter names, read “Ha-Mim.” They are among the disconnected letters, “huruf muqatta'at.” No authentic Prophetic report gives a definite hidden meaning, so certainty should stop where revelation stops.\n\nAs in the preceding chapters, mention of the Quran follows immediately. Commentators often observe that the Book is composed of familiar Arabic letters yet remains beyond human imitation in its complete form and guidance. This contextual connection is valuable, but it does not claim to exhaust Allah’s purpose in the letters.\n\nThe chapter will join revelation with purposeful creation, challenge idols, discuss testimony from the Children of Israel, command goodness to parents, recount the people of ’Ȃd, and describe jinn hearing the Quran. The quiet letter names introduce a wide argument about who responds to divine speech.\n\nTheir repeated appearance across seven chapters creates unity without erasing each chapter’s identity. A listener recognizes the family of themes while meeting a new arrangement and emphasis. The proper response remains disciplined humility: recite accurately, study reliable patterns, avoid secret-number theories, and move toward the clear statement that the Book comes from the Almighty, All-Wise. Unknown detail should increase reverence, not become an invitation to invent knowledge. The letters also link the final chapter in the sequence back to chapter 40, where the group began with revelation, forgiveness, and judgment. Across the seven chapters, opponents repeat accusations while Allah repeats evidence with varied emphasis. Repetition serves remembrance rather than mechanical duplication. Human hearts need truth approached through creation, history, law, mercy, and warning. The same two sounds introduce each new lesson, training the listener to recognize unity in a Book whose parts illuminate one another. With humility, the listener can enter that unity without pretending to know what Allah kept hidden.",
  "2": "“The revelation of this Book is from Allah” repeats the source stated in the previous chapter. Repetition is not unnecessary; the Quran’s divine origin is the foundation of every coming command and warning. Muhammad receives and conveys. The Book is not his literary project or borrowed history.\n\n“Revelation” indicates sending down from the Lord’s knowledge and authority into human language. Its Arabic form is the exact Quran, while translations communicate meanings. A translation may be helpful or flawed and should be improved, but a human wording does not replace the revealed original.\n\n“The Almighty” means Allah cannot be defeated by idols, opponents, or the passage of time. His Book survives human plots by His will. “All-Wise” means its arguments, laws, stories, and sequence carry purpose. The chapter will ask people to look at creation for a purpose and appointed term; that wisdom begins in the source of the Book explaining it.\n\nA believer approaches revelation with confidence and care. Might prevents fear that truth depends on human approval. Wisdom prevents crude use of truth for ego or harm. Questions can be studied because the source is not threatened by inquiry, while obedience remains due because temporary confusion does not make the Wise speaker mistaken. The two names produce firm, thoughtful submission. The repeated pair of names also answers two opposite fears. Someone may believe guidance is wise but too weak to survive opposition; “Almighty” removes that fear. Another may admire power but fear arbitrary rule; “All-Wise” removes that fear. Revelation comes from One whose plan cannot fail and whose plan never lacks right purpose. This gives the Prophet patience and gives readers a reason to remain steady when immediate events seem neither powerful nor wise from their limited viewpoint. Both qualities always remain the believer’s security, producing confidence joined with care.",
  "3": "“We only created the heavens and the earth and everything in between for a purpose” rejects the idea of an aimless universe. Creation displays truth, wisdom, service, testing, and signs of the Creator. Human life inside it therefore carries moral weight. Joy and rest have lawful places, but existence as a whole is not sport.\n\n“And an appointed term” means the current order has a fixed end. Stars, earth, nations, and individual lives do not continue independently forever. Allah knows the term and brings it at the right time. Hidden timing should produce readiness, not predictions unsupported by revelation.\n\n“The disbelievers are turning away from what they have been warned about” contrasts purposeful reality with chosen distraction. The warning concerns death, resurrection, and consequences. Turning away does not refute it. It redirects attention because accepting it would require changed worship and conduct.\n\nPurpose and term together give balance. A purposeful world should be studied, cultivated, and treated responsibly; an ending world should not be worshipped. Environmental care, justice, family duty, and lawful work matter precisely because life is a trust. Their limited duration does not make them meaningless. The Quran asks people to live the term according to its purpose rather than use the term’s delay as an excuse to ignore the warning. An appointed term also places civilizations under the same rule as individuals. A culture may feel permanent because it outlives one generation, yet it too has beginnings, changes, and an end known to Allah. This should make collective planning humble. Build institutions for long benefit, but do not use longevity as proof of moral correctness. The warning can be turned from before the term closes, and purposeful reform can give the limited years of a society real value before its record is complete. The term will arrive exactly.",
  "4": "“Have you considered whatever idols you invoke besides Allah?” asks for serious examination rather than inherited emotion. “Invoke” points to prayer and sacred calling, the heart of worship. The objects may be images, angels, saints, or imagined powers. The question is what real divine basis any possesses.\n\n“Show me what they have created on earth” demands evidence from creation. If they deserve worship, what independent work belongs to them? “Or do they have a share in the creation of the heavens?” removes the possibility of partnership. The pagans themselves admitted Allah created, so their worship had no support from the visible world.\n\n“Bring me a scripture revealed before this Quran” asks for revealed authority. No true messenger taught partners with Allah. “Or a shred of knowledge” broadens the challenge to any reliable trace, transmitted proof, or sound evidence. Strong custom and confident speech are not knowledge.\n\n“If what you say is true” places the burden on the claimant. Worship is too serious to build on imagination. The verse models clear reasoning: inspect creation, consult authentic revelation, and request knowledge. It does not say every ordinary practice needs a scripture, but beliefs about divinity and acts of worship do. When all three sources fail to support idols, continuing to invoke them is not loyalty; it is refusal to follow evidence. The threefold challenge also teaches standards of proof. Observation addresses what they claim about creative power; revelation addresses what they claim about divine approval; transmitted knowledge addresses any reliable legacy. Their position fails every route. Believers should welcome the same discipline for claims made in Islam’s name. A practice should not be called commanded merely because it is old or emotionally powerful. Clear scripture, authentic Sunnah, sound reasoning, and qualified scholarship protect worship from invention and protect people from leaders who claim sacred authority without evidence.",
  "5": "“Who could be more astray” asks for an extreme example of losing direction. Calling upon a being that cannot answer is not a harmless alternative route; it redirects worship from the One who hears. The question follows the absence of any creative, scriptural, or rational proof.\n\n“Those who call upon others besides Allah” concerns invocation seeking unseen divine help. Asking a living person for something within normal ability is not what the verse condemns. The wrong is treating creation as able to hear every prayer, control destiny, or grant what only Allah owns.\n\n“Cannot respond to them until the Day of Judgment” means they will never respond during the whole remaining period. “Until” sets the farthest horizon without implying a helpful answer afterward. “They are even unaware of their calls” applies clearly to lifeless idols and to righteous beings who did not know people were invoking them.\n\nThe verse brings devotion back to reality. Allah hears and responds with wisdom; false gods may not even know the call occurred. Emotional attachment cannot supply awareness or power. A person may honor prophets and righteous people without addressing worship to them. The most lost prayer is one aimed where no answer can ever come, while the most direct refuge is the Creator who knows the need before the tongue expresses it. “Until the Day of Judgment” also shows how long the caller may wait. A lifetime of repeated invocation does not give an unaware object awareness. Time cannot create divine qualities. This is especially important when people say generations have called upon a figure, so the practice must carry truth. Longevity proves only repetition. The Day will reveal whether any response existed. Directing prayer to Allah avoids that tragic delay because the All-Hearing knows every language, whisper, need, and sincere hope now.",
  "6": "“When such people will be gathered together” moves the issue of false worship to Judgment Day. Worshippers and those they treated as gods will appear under Allah’s authority. Gathering removes myths protected by distance and death. Every relationship is seen in truth.\n\n“Those gods will be their enemies” means the falsely worshipped objects or beings become a source of hostility rather than rescue. Lifeless idols testify by Allah’s power, evil beings reject loyalty, and righteous servants disapprove worship they never requested. The very attachment expected to help becomes evidence against the worshipper.\n\n“They will disown their worship” shows complete separation. Jesus, angels, and saints do not accept acts directed to them against Allah’s command. Evil leaders may also abandon followers. A name invoked repeatedly in worldly life provides no contract over the being named.\n\nThis scene clarifies intercession. Valid intercession occurs only by Allah’s permission and never because someone worshipped the intercessor. False worship produces disowning, not favored access. The living should test religious love now: does it follow the honored servant’s actual teaching and remain within “tawhid”? Proper love for prophets leads to Allah; exaggerated love that gives them His rights turns future friendship into enmity. Gathering will reveal the distinction, but revelation allows it to be understood before then. The future enmity also reveals that worship can wrong the one worshipped. Righteous servants love Allah and would hate being placed beside Him. Claiming devotion to them while violating their deepest teaching is not honor. Christians do not honor Jesus by making him divine, and Muslims would not honor Muhammad by calling upon him as an independent lord. True love protects the Creator-servant boundary and hopes to meet the messenger as a follower rather than as someone whose excess he must disown. That truthful distinction protects sincere love forever.",
  "7": "“Whenever Our clear revelations are recited to them” shows repeated and understandable delivery. The Quran comes as recitation, engaging hearing and reason. “Clear” means its central message and proof are made plain, not that every human interpretation is correct or no study is needed.\n\n“The disbelievers say of the truth when it has come to them” first names the revelation by its reality: truth. Their label does not change it. “When it has come” emphasizes access and immediacy. They are not judging a distant rumor but words being presented.\n\n“This is pure magic” tries to explain the Quran’s effect while avoiding its source. Magic suggests manipulation and illusion, yet the Book invites open reflection and consistent worship. The accusation gives no answer to its argument, the Prophet’s character, or its moral coherence.\n\nThe repeated “whenever” suggests a fixed response regardless of content. That reveals prejudice rather than evaluation. A loaded label can make an audience stop listening before evidence begins. Believers should not copy this method when confronting other claims. They should represent accurately, ask for proof, and respond fairly. Truth does not fear examination, and falsehood is not defeated by replacing one dismissive label with another. The Quran’s clarity deserves a hearing on its own terms. The phrase “when it has come to them” also removes the comfort of endless postponement. Truth has arrived before the label is spoken. The audience’s task is present: listen, compare, and decide honestly. A person can take time to learn, but cannot call avoidance investigation. Regularly changing accusations—magic, fabrication, ancient tale—may reveal that no answer would be accepted. The Quran’s stable message against changing labels invites the seeker to examine which side has real coherence. The truth itself remains unchanged. Every time it is recited. Labels eventually fade, but revealed truth continues calling people toward honest examination.",
  "8": "“He has fabricated this Quran” is the opponents’ accusation that Muhammad knowingly invented speech and attributed it to Allah. Fabrication against God would be an enormous sin, so the Quran answers with the consequence rather than treating it lightly.\n\n“If I have done so, then there is nothing whatsoever you can do to save me from Allah” tells the Prophet to admit that no human support could protect a liar in revelation. His willingness to place himself under that judgment demonstrates sincerity. The accusers themselves could neither shield nor punish independently of Allah.\n\n“He knows best what slurs you indulge about it” means Allah fully knows their speculative talk, distortions, and mockery. “Sufficient is He as a Witness between you and me” places the disputed claim before perfect knowledge. The Prophet still presents evidence, but final testimony does not depend on winning their approval.\n\n“He is the All-Forgiving, Most Merciful” unexpectedly leaves the door open. Even slanderers can repent before death. Divine witness guarantees justice, while these names invite return. A believer falsely accused can trust Allah without becoming cruel. The verse combines moral seriousness, confidence under accusation, and hope that opponents may leave the slur and receive forgiveness. Forgiveness and mercy at the end also correct anyone who thinks firm defense of revelation requires closing the door on accusers. The Prophet answers the charge clearly, entrusts judgment to Allah, and still names qualities of pardon. This is moral confidence. It can confront a lie without treating the liar as permanently beyond guidance. Believers should defend the Prophet with evidence and dignity, hoping that someone repeating a slur may later recognize the Witness, repent, and become a sincere defender of the truth once attacked. Mercy still leaves an honorable road back today, even after a grave accusation has been answered.",
  "9": "“I am not the first messenger ever sent” places Muhammad within known prophetic history. Noah, Abraham, Moses, Jesus, and many others came before him. His humanity, opposition, and call to one God are not strange innovations. The Quran asks listeners to compare his mission with that consistent line.\n\n“Nor do I know what will happen to me or you” refers to worldly details and outcomes except what Allah reveals. The Prophet did not independently know the unseen. Later revelation gives him certainty of Allah’s favor and final reward, so the phrase cannot mean doubt about every ultimate promise. It denies self-owned knowledge of future events.\n\n“I only follow what is revealed to me” defines prophetic authority and humility. Muhammad does not invent signs or policy against revelation. “I am only sent with a clear warning” emphasizes delivery: he makes danger plain but cannot force hearts or set judgment’s date.\n\nThis verse protects Muslims from exaggerating the Prophet. He is the greatest human messenger but remains Allah’s servant, knowing unseen matters only when taught. Following him therefore means following revelation, not assigning divine qualities to him. It also comforts callers: they are not responsible for controlling outcomes. Clear, faithful delivery and obedient example are their task; future results belong to the Lord. The phrase also places future predictions under discipline. Claims that the Prophet knows every hidden event independently contradict his own declaration. Authentic prophecies are true because Allah revealed them, not because prophethood turns a human being into an all-knowing god. This distinction strengthens rather than reduces Muhammad’s honor. He is perfectly faithful to what he is given and perfectly honest about what he is not given. Servanthood makes his testimony trustworthy because he never claims the Lord’s exclusive qualities. Clear limits are always part of presenting truthful prophethood faithfully and without exaggeration.",
  "10": "“Consider if this Quran is truly from Allah and you deny it” enters the opponents’ position to expose its danger. The “if” does not show doubt from Allah; it asks rejecters to consider the consequence of their own uncertainty. Dismissing a claim of revelation without fair examination is reckless when the evidence and stakes are so great.\n\n“A witness from the Children of Israel attests to it and then believes” has been connected by many commentators to Abdullah ibn Salam, a learned Jewish man who accepted Islam. Because the chapter is generally Meccan, other scholars understand the witness more broadly or compare Moses’ scripture with the Quran. The core meaning is that informed testimony from Israel recognizes the message’s continuity.\n\n“Whereas you act arrogantly” contrasts a knowledgeable outsider’s acceptance with the Meccans’ pride. Their Arab identity and closeness to the Prophet did not produce humility. Evidence can be received by someone socially distant and rejected by those who assume they should have been first.\n\n“Allah does not guide the wrongdoing people” warns that arrogance and denial are moral wrongs that block guidance. It does not say a present wrongdoer can never repent. The very question is an invitation to stop. Guidance requires willingness to learn even when another community’s witness reaches the truth before one’s own leaders. The witness interpretation also shows that knowledge creates responsibility across communities. A person familiar with earlier scripture may recognize patterns another misses, while a person with the Quran must evaluate that testimony by final revelation. Interfaith conversation is most useful when sources are represented accurately and truth matters more than group victory. The Meccans’ arrogance prevented them from valuing a witness outside their elite. Humility allows evidence to come through an unexpected person without making that person an independent authority over Allah’s Book.",
  "11": "“Had it been something good, they would not have beaten us to it” reports the disbelievers judging truth by social rank. Poor, enslaved, or less influential people often accepted Islam before elites. The elites concluded that anything embraced by such people could not be good. Their pride treated privilege as a guarantee of better judgment.\n\nThe reasoning is circular: we are superior, so we would recognize good first; because others arrived first, it cannot be good. It never examines the Quran itself. History repeatedly shows that comfort can delay reform, while people with fewer investments in an unjust order may hear more openly. Wealth is not itself blameworthy, but it does not grant spiritual insight.\n\n“Since they reject its guidance” identifies the real condition. “They will say, ‘This is an ancient fabrication!’” gives another changing label. Earlier tradition is used when useful and dismissed as old invention when it supports Muhammad. The accusation again avoids evidence.\n\nThe verse corrects class prejudice within Muslim communities too. A truth does not become lower because a poor person states it, and a wealthy scholar is not wrong merely for wealth. Judge proof and character. Being “beaten” to good should produce gratitude and willingness to follow, not resentment. The right response to someone reaching truth first is to join, because guidance is not a race whose value disappears when another wins the first step. The same pride can appear after a religious movement becomes established. Later members may assume truth always reaches people of their class, school, or ethnicity first. This verse keeps the door of humility open. Allah guides whom He wills, and a new believer may recognize and practice a neglected truth before someone with years of inherited identity. The proper response is not to protect rank but to welcome good, verify it, and thank Allah that another servant reached it.",
  "12": "“Before this Quran the Book of Moses was revealed as a guide and mercy” affirms the original Torah’s divine source. It guided the Children of Israel and brought merciful law and teaching. The Quran does not present Muhammad as disconnected from Moses. Both Books come from Allah and call to worship Him.\n\n“This Book is a confirmation” means the Quran confirms the essential truth of earlier revelation and the prophetic pattern. It also serves as the final authority over later claims, correcting distortion and clarifying disagreement. Confirmation does not require every legal detail to remain identical; different prophetic laws can share one divine foundation.\n\n“In the Arabic tongue” makes it directly understandable to the first audience. Language serves communication, not ethnic superiority. “To warn those who do wrong” gives one function, while “as good news to those who do good” gives the balance. Warning protects from consequence; good news strengthens faithful action.\n\nThe phrase “those who do good” includes people whose belief produces excellence toward Allah and creation. The Quran’s two voices meet different needs in the same heart: fear restrains wrong and hope supports perseverance. A Book that only comforts could feed carelessness; one heard only as threat could produce despair. Confirmation, warning, and good news together carry the mercy already present in Moses’ genuine scripture. Confirmation also gives a rule for reading shared prophetic stories. Similarity between the Quran and earlier scripture is not proof of copying; it is expected when the true source is one. Differences are examined through the Quran’s role as final criterion and through reliable history, not used casually to insult another community. The Arabic Book makes the message accessible to a new first audience while preserving honor for Moses and every genuine revelation that preceded it. The one source explains both continuity and correction.",
  "13": "“Our Lord is Allah” is the declaration at the center of faith. It recognizes Allah as Creator, owner, nurturer, and only object of worship. The speaker rejects rival lords over conscience and turns every sacred act toward Him. Words are essential, but the verse immediately asks what follows them.\n\n“Then remain steadfast” describes “istiqamah,” continuing upright upon belief and obedience. It is not sinless perfection. A steadfast person can stumble, then repent and return rather than abandon the path. Steadfastness covers prayer, character, patience, and consistency when social pressure changes.\n\n“There will be no fear for them” promises final safety regarding what lies ahead. “Nor will they grieve” removes lasting sorrow over what passed. Believers may feel fear and grief in worldly life; prophets did. The promise concerns ultimate condition, especially at death and judgment, when Allah’s security becomes complete.\n\nThe verse resembles chapter 41, verse 30, where angels descend upon people who make the same declaration and remain steadfast. The Quran explains itself: final freedom from fear grows from a life that keeps Allah as Lord through change. One declaration begins the journey; repeated faithful choices establish it. Mercy, not personal perfection, brings the promised end, while steadfastness is the lived evidence of sincere words. Steadfastness is especially visible when conditions reverse. A person may speak faith during hardship and forget in relief, or practice during social approval and withdraw under ridicule. “Then remain steadfast” asks for one Lord through both conditions. This does not forbid adapting lawful methods or acknowledging exhaustion. Rest, counsel, and support help constancy. The path remains the same even when pace changes. Allah’s promise addresses the person whose direction survives human weakness through repeated return. Steadfast servants keep the declaration alive through patience, repentance, worship, and service until final fear and grief are removed.",
  "14": "“It is they who will be the residents of Paradise” identifies those who declare Allah as Lord and remain steadfast. “Residents” implies a settled home, not a temporary visit. They belong there by Allah’s welcome and will not face expulsion, insecurity, or death.\n\n“Staying there forever” makes permanence explicit. Every worldly home is left through travel, loss, or death. Paradise ends that instability. Eternity does not produce boredom because its pleasures, relationships, and nearness to Allah contain no defect or exhaustion. Present experience cannot measure the full nature of everlasting life.\n\n“As a reward for what they used to do” affirms the importance of continuing action. Their deeds did not force Allah to pay a debt; ability, guidance, and acceptance came from Him. Authentic teaching makes mercy the basis of entry. Yet Allah graciously connects reward to faith expressed through deeds, giving human choice real significance.\n\n“Used to do” points to habits across life, including repeated repentance after failure. One heroic moment does not replace the steady path, and one regretted failure does not erase it when sincerely repaired. The verse encourages durable goodness: prayer maintained, rights honored, and character corrected. Such work is temporary, but Allah turns it by grace into a home whose residence is forever. Eternal residence also answers the human search for belonging. Worldly homes can be loving but remain vulnerable; some people never experience a safe home at all. Paradise is not awarded according to property ownership, family status, or whether earthly housing was secure. Allah grants a final residence through faith and mercy. This can comfort displaced and lonely believers while motivating communities to provide shelter and dignity now. Hope in an eternal home should increase concern for people denied a safe temporary one. Eternal belonging, forever safe by the Lord’s mercy, is among His greatest gifts.",
  "15": "“We have commanded people to honour their parents” gives a universal duty after steadfast faith. Honoring includes respectful speech, service, gratitude, care in old age, and lawful obedience. It does not require obeying a parent in sin or remaining exposed to abuse. Boundaries can be necessary while respect and justice are preserved according to ability.\n\n“Their mothers bore them in hardship and delivered them in hardship” names the physical burden often hidden after birth. Pregnancy changes the body, carries risk, and ends in labor. Mentioning the mother’s hardship gives special weight to gratitude without reducing the father’s rights. “Bearing and weaning is thirty months” joins pregnancy and nursing; together with chapter 2’s two years of nursing, scholars derive a minimum pregnancy of six months.\n\n“At the age of forty” represents mature strength and reflection. The person prays for gratitude toward Allah’s favors upon self and parents, good deeds that please Him, and righteous offspring. The prayer links three generations and turns maturity toward responsibility rather than self-satisfaction.\n\n“I truly repent to You, and I truly submit” prevents family goodness from becoming pride. Even a grateful adult needs repentance and surrender. The verse offers a lifelong pattern: honor the past through parents, use present strength in pleasing deeds, care about the future through prayer for offspring, and keep the whole family story under Allah’s mercy. The prayer at forty is not restricted to people who reach exactly that birthday before becoming grateful. It presents a point of full maturity and invites the prayer throughout adult life. Someone older can still repent, thank, and begin righteousness; someone younger can learn its direction early. The wording also recognizes that caring for parents may become more demanding as the child reaches mature responsibilities. Gratitude is not a childhood duty that expires but grows deeper as one understands what bearing, provision, and upbringing required.",
  "16": "“It is from these people that We will accept the good they did” refers to those who reach maturity with gratitude, good deeds, care for parents, prayer for offspring, repentance, and submission. Acceptance is greater than merely performing an act. A deed can be outwardly good while harmed by showing off or injustice; Allah knows and accepts sincere good by grace.\n\n“And overlook their misdeeds” balances hope. Mature believers carry a history of shortcomings, but repentance and divine pardon prevent those failures from defining the final account. “Overlook” does not call wrongdoing harmless. It displays mercy toward people who acknowledge and return. Rights owed to others still require repair.\n\n“Along with the residents of Paradise” identifies their final company. Honoring parents and caring for descendants belong within the wider faith leading to Paradise. Family duty is not a secondary cultural decoration; it is a major expression of submission. Yet a person without children or with difficult family circumstances can still fulfill duties according to the situation and seek the same mercy.\n\n“In fulfilment of the true promise” assures that Allah never breaks His word. The promise is true in source and outcome. Believers do not demand acceptance based on self-worth; they trust the Lord who promised to accept and pardon this sincere pattern. That certainty encourages effort without despair: good can be accepted, wrong can be overlooked, and a flawed human life can end among Paradise’s residents. Acceptance and overlooking also show how Allah judges a whole life. He is not looking for a technical reason to reject every imperfect deed. He accepts genuine good and pardons failures according to repentance and mercy. This should not create complacency; it creates loving effort. The servant wants to bring deeds worth accepting while knowing their defects. When people judge each other, they should remember this generosity and avoid reducing a repentant person to the worst chapter of a record Allah may overlook.",
  "17": "“Some scold their parents” presents the opposite family response. The child’s harsh “Enough with you!” shows contempt toward parents who are trying to save him. Disagreement about belief does not justify humiliation, especially toward those owed gratitude. The sin combines rejection of resurrection with cruel speech.\n\n“Are you warning me that I will be brought forth” mocks resurrection by pointing to generations that perished. Their long absence is treated as proof they are gone forever, though delay says nothing about Allah’s ability or appointed time. The argument repeats the demand for forefathers while adding family conflict.\n\n“The parents cry to Allah for help” shows love and dependence. They warn, “Have faith! Surely Allah’s promise is true,” but cannot force the heart. Their prayer models parents facing an adult child’s rejection: advise sincerely, ask Allah, and recognize the limit of control.\n\n“This is nothing but ancient fables” is the child’s final dismissal. Reports attempting to assign this verse to a particular Companion were rejected by Aisha in the case of Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr; the wording is general. It portrays a recurring type, not permission to stain a named person. The tragedy is not simply youthful doubt but arrogant persistence against loving counsel and Allah’s promise. The parents’ method combines prayer and direct counsel. They do not rely only on argument or only on private supplication. They cry to Allah and say, “Have faith.” Parents facing disbelief should learn sound answers, maintain relationship where safe, and keep asking guidance. Yet they must not become abusive in the name of preventing the child’s abuse. Allah guides hearts. The parents fulfill love through truth, patience, and prayer, while the adult child remains responsible for the contempt and choice. Their loving warning remains successful as obedience even when the child chooses not to receive it.",
  "18": "“These are the ones” refers to the rejecting type just described, not every child who asks a question or struggles temporarily. The plural confirms the passage gives a general pattern. They mock resurrection, insult believing parents, and dismiss revelation as ancient fables without proof.\n\n“Against whom the fate of earlier communities of jinn and humans has been justified” places them within a history of morally responsible beings. Jinn and humans both receive guidance and face judgment. “Justified” means the promised sentence becomes deserved and applicable after chosen rejection; Allah does not force innocence into loss.\n\nEarlier communities provide warning, not an excuse. A person may say many before me believed the same, but numbers across history do not make falsehood safe. Shared denial creates shared outcome while each individual record remains exact. Jinn are mentioned to widen the account beyond visible humanity without inviting speculation about their details.\n\n“They were truly losers” defines the result. They lose parental goodness, guidance, their own souls, and lasting reward. Worldly independence may make rejection feel like freedom, but final loss reveals its cost. Living listeners have not yet reached the fixed fate. Faith, respectful repair with parents, and repentance can move a person from the described group. The warning names the road so it can be left before its sentence is justified. Mention of jinn also prepares for the later scene in this chapter when a group of jinn hears the Quran and believes. The contrast is powerful: an arrogant human child dismisses revelation as fables, while unseen listeners encounter the same Book and return as warners to their people. Species and background do not determine response. Humility does. Both jinn and humans can lose through rejection or receive guidance through hearing, and Allah judges each community according to the message and choices before it.",
  "19": "“Each of the two groups will be ranked” refers to the grateful, repentant believers and the defiant rejecters described in the preceding family examples. Judgment is not only a simple division into two groups; within each are degrees reflecting faith, sincerity, harm, patience, and action. Allah knows distinctions hidden from people.\n\n“According to what they have done” links rank to real response. Family background, claims, and social status do not determine the level. The believing child’s honor does not automatically save a rejecting child, and difficult parents do not prevent a faithful person from rising. Each deed and intention is known.\n\n“So He may fully reward all” means no part of recompense is left incomplete. “Reward” includes favorable or unfavorable payment according to context. Good is multiplied through grace, while evil is judged with justice. Repentance can remove evil before the final calculation.\n\n“None will be wronged” is the foundation under every rank. Allah does not lower one person to elevate another or confuse communal records. This should stop envy about another believer’s degree and stop confidence that influence will reduce guilt. Human beings should pursue the highest faithful response while asking mercy, not compare themselves proudly. The complete ranking belongs to a Judge who knows every opportunity, burden, and choice and treats no soul unfairly. Degrees also reveal Allah’s generosity in recognizing differences among good deeds. A person with limited ability may rise through sincerity, while a greater outward act may carry weaker intention. Likewise, wrongdoing differs in knowledge, persistence, and harm. Humans should not create precise heavenly rankings from appearances. Their task is to strive and judge earthly matters by available evidence. Allah completes what they cannot see. “None will be wronged” means every subtle difference receives exactly the understanding and mercy it deserves. Perfect justice preserves every meaningful difference while allowing no unfairness at all.",
  "20": "“The Day when the disbelievers will be exposed to the Fire” brings final consequence into view. “Exposed” means made to face the reality they denied, before entering punishment. The scene establishes truth openly and leaves no hiding place.\n\n“You already exhausted your share of pleasures during your worldly life” does not mean every disbeliever received every possible good or that believers must reject lawful enjoyment. It condemns people whose good aim and reward were limited to worldly desire. They consumed gifts without gratitude or eternal investment, so nothing saved remains for the Hereafter.\n\n“Today you will be rewarded with the torment of disgrace” fits their “arrogance throughout the land with no right.” They sought elevation through rejecting Allah and looking down on people. Forced disgrace answers that false greatness. “For your rebelliousness” adds repeated disobedience to inward pride.\n\nThe verse warns wealthy and poor alike because worldliness is a direction, not an amount. A person with little can make the world the only goal, while a wealthy believer can enjoy, give, and seek Allah. Lawful pleasures become lasting when joined to gratitude and good. The condemned exhausted theirs by letting pleasure, arrogance, and rebellion consume the whole account. The listener can still redirect enjoyment into obedience before exposure to the Fire. Exhausting pleasure also warns against consuming every gift on the self. Charity, hospitality, care for dependents, and public benefit turn provision outward and seek reward beyond death. Ascetic performance without faith is not the answer, and Islam does not forbid beauty. The question is whether pleasure remains servant or becomes lord. Arrogance says the gift proves superiority; gratitude says the gift creates duty. One ends in disgrace, while the other can make the same worldly resource part of an eternal share. Enjoyment becomes worship when gratitude sends some of its good beyond the self.",
  "21": "“Remember the brother of ’Ȃd” refers to the prophet Hud, a member of the people he warned. Calling him their brother emphasizes shared humanity and tribal connection, not agreement with idolatry. He knew their language and conditions and wanted their rescue. A messenger from among them removed the excuse that guidance was foreign.\n\n“Who inhabited the sand-hills” locates ’Ȃd in “al-Ahqaf,” curved or extended sand dunes, commonly associated with southern Arabia. Exact archaeological identifications should not be asserted without reliable proof. The geographical image matters because the people were established in a landscape the Arabs could understand.\n\n“There were certainly warners before and after him” places Hud in prophetic continuity. His central words, “Worship none but Allah,” were not a new tribal idea. Messengers across time carried the same “tawhid.” “I truly fear for you the torment of a tremendous day” shows compassionate warning, not desire for destruction.\n\nHud’s relationship to his people models sincere calling. He does not claim superiority through foreign identity or hide the danger to remain popular. Being a “brother” makes truth more caring, not less firm. Communities should hear correction from trustworthy members without treating loyalty as silence. Real brotherhood warns when worship and conduct lead toward a tremendous account. Hud is also called “brother” before his people accept him, showing that social relationship can remain while theological difference is stated clearly. Believers need not deny shared ancestry, language, or civic concern in order to reject false worship. Those bonds can become avenues of mercy and honest service. Yet brotherhood cannot require silence about ultimate danger. Hud’s love appears precisely in his willingness to risk rejection by naming the torment he fears for those closest to him. Loving truth for one’s people is deeper loyalty than protecting their most dangerous error. That is prophetic concern.",
  "22": "“Have you come to turn us away from our gods?” frames Hud’s call as harmful interference rather than liberation from false worship. The people define identity through inherited gods, so evidence against them feels like an attack on the community. Their question avoids asking whether those gods create, hear, or possess scriptural authority.\n\n“Bring us then whatever you threaten us with” moves from defense to challenge. They demand punishment as proof, assuming delay shows inability. A warning is meant to help them avoid the event; asking to hasten it reverses mercy into mockery. The messenger does not control timing, as his answer explains.\n\n“If what you say is true” makes truth depend on meeting their reckless schedule. Yet a delayed judgment can be certain, and Allah may delay to allow repentance. Their standard would punish them rather than educate them. The demand reveals confidence built on present strength.\n\nThe verse warns against turning every unwelcome correction into an attack on culture. Tradition deserves respect where sound, but no identity makes idols true. It also teaches people not to demand harm in order to test a warning. Examine its evidence while safety remains. Hud’s people could have asked how to return; they asked for what he feared. That choice exposes how pride can prefer destructive proof over humble reconsideration. Their demand also tries to transfer the burden of proof incorrectly. Hud has brought the prophetic message and signs; they respond by demanding the threatened result instead of supporting their gods. A fair defense would show creative power or revelation for those gods, but none is offered. In debate, dramatic counter-demands often hide this absence. The listener should return to the original question: what evidence makes worship of these beings true, and why has the Creator’s call been refused? A merciful warning should be examined, not mocked into becoming a requested disaster.",
  "23": "“The knowledge of its time is only with Allah” places punishment’s schedule outside Hud’s control. A prophet announces what Allah reveals but does not own the unseen. The answer refuses both date-setting and the demand for spectacle. Delay cannot be interpreted as falsehood when the Lord intentionally keeps timing.\n\n“I only convey to you what I have been sent with” defines Hud’s responsibility. He speaks neither less to please them nor more to impress them. “Sent with” means content and authority come from Allah. The messenger cannot replace delivery with forced belief.\n\n“You are a people acting ignorantly” describes conduct that ignores evidence and requests destruction. They may possess worldly skill, but ignorance here is moral foolishness: worshipping powerless gods and mistaking patience for weakness. Hud speaks directly after patient warning because false confidence has become dangerous.\n\nThe verse gives callers a boundary. Explain clearly, admit what Allah has not disclosed, and do not invent dates or hidden details to satisfy pressure. A teacher who says “I do not know” where evidence ends protects trust. It also gives listeners responsibility: lack of a timetable does not answer a warning supported by truth. Wisdom uses the delay to return rather than acting ignorantly until the unknown time becomes present. Calling them ignorant is therefore diagnosis, not mockery of education. A society can understand architecture, weather, and trade while remaining ignorant of the One who gives those abilities. Knowledge becomes whole when it recognizes purpose and moral boundary. Hud does not ask them to discard useful skill. He asks them to stop letting skill create false confidence. The unknown timing itself should humble them: mastery of some processes has not given control over tomorrow, death, or Allah’s final command. Humility uses the unknown time for repentance rather than for another display of confidence.",
  "24": "“They saw the torment as a dense cloud approaching their valleys” shows the warning entering their familiar landscape. A large cloud normally promised rain to a people needing water. Its density and direction created hope because they interpreted only through desire. The same visible sign carried a meaning they did not expect.\n\n“This is a cloud bringing us rain” is a natural conclusion in ordinary conditions, but revelation had given this moment a moral context. Their confidence illustrates how people read events according to wishes. What appears as relief may contain consequence, and what feels difficult may carry mercy. Humans should avoid claiming hidden meanings without revelation, yet remain humble about first impressions.\n\n“No, it is what you sought to hasten” connects the cloud directly to their challenge. “A fierce wind carrying a painful punishment” replaces hoped-for water with destructive air. The wind was created, commanded, and unable to act independently. ’Ȃd’s strength could not withstand an ordinary element when Allah directed it.\n\nThe scene should not make people call every storm divine punishment. This event is identified by revelation. Its enduring lesson is about proud misreading and delayed warning. Hud feared the Day for them; they demanded it; then its first appearance looked like good news. A believer asks Allah for beneficial rain and wind, uses forecasts responsibly, and remembers that created forces remain under the Lord. The valley setting makes their expectation understandable and their error more moving. They knew clouds as signs of needed rain and likely watched the horizon eagerly. Yet previous warnings should have made them cautious about challenging Allah. Revelation does not teach believers to fear every cloud. It teaches them to avoid interpreting every favorable appearance as divine approval. Gratitude during relief and repentance during warning are safer than making desire the only lens through which unfolding events are read.",
  "25": "“It destroyed everything by the command of its Lord” means the wind devastated everything Allah decreed within its path, especially the people and structures under judgment. “Everything” is understood by context, not as destruction of the entire planet. The wind itself has a Lord and follows command, so nature is neither independent nor divine.\n\n“Leaving nothing visible except their ruins” turns a powerful civilization into empty remains. Bodies, homes, or traces testified that worldly strength had ended. The exact archaeological site should not be claimed with certainty, but the moral ruin is clear: the society that expected rain became a lesson in dependence.\n\n“This is how We reward the wicked people” uses “reward” as fitting repayment. Wickedness included idolatry, arrogance, rejection, and demanding punishment. Allah did not destroy them without messengers or proof. The result follows a known pattern of justice.\n\nThe verse’s severity is meant to prevent repetition. Modern construction may resist stronger winds than ancient homes, but no technology leaves Allah’s kingdom. Responsible engineering is required, not mocked; it is one created means of safety. The warning concerns making means a reason for spiritual independence. Ruins show that tools can fail, while repentance and humility remain protections whose full value reaches beyond every physical structure. “By the command of its Lord” also prevents superstition toward storms. Wind has no moral intention and deserves no worship. Allah can make it mercy, transport, pollination, cooling, or punishment. People should study and prepare for severe weather, aid victims, and avoid announcing hidden guilt. In the specific case of ’Ȃd, revelation supplies the moral meaning. In ordinary disasters, compassion and practical help come first, while everyone may humbly remember dependence and examine personal conduct without accusing sufferers. The strongest visible shelter remains temporary, while obedience prepares safety beyond every storm. No structure is absolute.",
  "26": "“We had established them in a way We have not established you Meccans” tells the first audience that ’Ȃd possessed greater strength, resources, or worldly position. The Meccans could not treat their own influence as immunity. History already contained people better established materially who disappeared after rejection.\n\n“We gave them hearing, sight, and intellect” names faculties for receiving evidence. Ears hear the messenger, eyes see signs, and hearts or intellects connect meaning. These gifts make knowledge possible and create responsibility. They are not valuable merely as physical functions if the person refuses their purpose.\n\n“Neither their hearing, sight, nor intellect were of any benefit” because they “persisted in denying Allah’s signs.” The problem was not weakness of the faculties but chosen use. Information passed through senses without becoming humble faith. Ability cannot benefit when directed to protect refusal.\n\n“They were overwhelmed by what they used to ridicule” gives the final reversal. The warning they treated as beneath serious thought surrounded them. The verse speaks strongly to educated and technically capable societies. Knowledge is a gift, not guaranteed guidance. It benefits spiritually when it accepts moral truth and its limits. Hearing, sight, and intellect reach their proper goal when they lead from signs to gratitude, worship, and responsible action. The three faculties also imply three duties: listen fairly, observe honestly, and reason humbly. Failure in one can corrupt the others. A person who refuses to hear may never consider the sign; one who sees only what confirms desire may misuse observation; one whose intellect serves pride may build elaborate excuses. Revelation does not oppose the senses or mind. It calls them back to their created purpose. Their benefit is not only producing worldly control but leading the servant toward truth. Faculties become saving blessings only when truth is allowed to guide their use.",
  "27": "“We certainly destroyed the societies around you” addresses Meccans who traveled past or knew about ruins of earlier peoples in Arabia and neighboring lands. Judgment was not a distant possibility known only through foreign stories. Their environment contained reminders of communities that once appeared settled.\n\n“After having varied the signs” means Allah presented evidence and warning in different forms: messengers, arguments, miracles, blessings, hardship, and historical outcomes. Variation answers different hearts and prevents the excuse that only one inaccessible kind of proof was offered. The Quran itself varies story, question, promise, and warning.\n\n“So perhaps they would return to the Right Path” gives the merciful aim before destruction. Signs were not sent to trap people. They invited return through repentance and worship. “Perhaps” addresses human response and possibility; Allah already knows outcomes.\n\nThe verse teaches varied, patient education. A caller may explain through reason, history, example, and compassion without changing the truth. It also warns listeners who continually demand another form while refusing each one received. Variety is an opportunity, not endless postponement. Societies should study nearby history with moral care, but not label every ruin a special punishment without revelation. The established examples are enough to show why return should happen before the last sign becomes consequence. Variation in signs also teaches that different people may need different starting points. One responds to creation, another to moral reasoning, another to history or personal mercy. Teachers can use varied doors without claiming there are different ultimate truths. Every sound door leads to worship of Allah and following His messenger. The mistake is changing the destination to keep an audience comfortable. Allah varied signs while preserving one call. Human education should similarly combine flexibility in method with faithfulness in meaning. Returning today remains the intended response to every varied sign before final consequence.",
  "28": "“Why then did those idols they took as gods —hoping to get closer to Him—not come to their aid?” asks where the promised spiritual protection went. The people worshipped intermediaries “hoping to get closer to Him,” assigning created objects a role Allah never authorized. Judgment tests the claim at the moment help matters most. No idol can stand between Creator and servant.\n\n“Instead, they failed them” means the supposed gods disappeared from their expected function. Some never had awareness; righteous beings never approved the worship; evil leaders could not rescue. The failure was built into the original claim, though comfort and tradition hid it.\n\n“That is the result of their lies and their fabrications” identifies invented theology as the source. They spoke of partners, closeness, and guaranteed help without creative evidence or scripture. Repetition across generations did not turn fabrication into truth.\n\nSeeking closeness to Allah is right, but the means must be authorized by Him: faith, prayer, repentance, good deeds, and following His messengers. Worshipping an intermediary creates distance while promising closeness. Lawful requests from living people and love of the righteous remain different from invocation. The verse asks believers to test every spiritual practice by revelation before a final crisis reveals that an imagined aid was never there. Fabrication about intermediaries often begins with a true desire—closeness to Allah—and a false method. This shows why good intention alone cannot make worship sound. Methods of devotion require revealed permission. The Prophet provides the lawful example: call Allah directly, seek His names, perform commanded worship, and love righteous people without giving them divine rights. A sincere goal needs a truthful road. Otherwise, imagined closeness ends as abandonment precisely when the worshipper expects the invented means to work. A lawful path to closeness must come from the One being approached. Revelation alone defines it faithfully. In every age. With complete sincerity.",
  "29": "“We sent a group of jinn your way to listen to the Quran” introduces unseen beings responding differently from many visible Meccans. Jinn are morally responsible creation, distinct from humans and normally hidden. Allah directed a group toward the Prophet’s recitation. Authentic reports indicate he did not initially know of their presence until revelation informed him, emphasizing Allah’s management of the encounter.\n\n“Listen quietly!” is their immediate instruction to one another. They create the conditions for understanding rather than drowning recitation in noise. Quiet attention is itself a moral act when Allah’s words are read. It allows the whole passage to be heard before judgment.\n\n“When it was over, they returned to their fellow jinn as warners” shows learning becoming service. They did not remain only fascinated by a supernatural experience. They understood enough to carry the message home, warning with concern. They were not new prophets; they conveyed what they heard as believing callers.\n\nThe scene gives the Meccans a humbling comparison. Unseen travelers hear once, listen respectfully, and act, while local opponents repeatedly mock. Background does not decide guidance. The verse also limits speculation: jinn are real, but their world should be described only through reliable revelation. The central lesson is attentive hearing followed by responsible sharing. Their role as warners also indicates that jinn communities have communication and moral responsibility, but the verse gives no basis for humans seeking secret contact with them. Attempts to summon jinn invite deception and distract from the Quran they themselves honored. The safest human response is to listen to the same revelation and follow its guidance. Extraordinary curiosity should not overshadow the ordinary miracle of a Book capable of reaching seen and unseen creation through Allah’s direction. Quiet, attentive listening always remains the sound response for every creation reached by the Quran.",
  "30": "“O our fellow jinn!” shows the listeners returning with belonging and concern. They address their own people rather than claiming private spiritual superiority. Guidance produces responsibility toward community. Their message begins with what they actually heard, not invented details about the Prophet.\n\n“A scripture revealed after Moses” shows their knowledge of earlier revelation. They mention Moses, perhaps because Torah law was especially familiar to them. Not mentioning Jesus does not deny him; the point is the Quran’s place in prophetic continuity. “Confirming what came before it” affirms the essential truth sent through earlier books.\n\n“It guides to the truth and the Straight Way” distinguishes content and direction. Truth corrects belief; the Straight Way orders life toward Allah. The jinn recognize the Quran not as entertainment but as guidance that demands response.\n\nTheir concise report models faithful teaching: identify the source, connect it accurately with earlier revelation, explain its truth, and call toward the path. They do not exaggerate personal status or build a movement around having heard secretly. A listener who benefits from Quran should likewise return to people with humility and accurate meaning. The miracle is not only that jinn listened; it is that listening immediately produced clear recognition and service. Their reference to Moses may also indicate that some jinn had encountered Jewish teaching or the Torah’s influence. The Quran does not describe every detail, and speculation is unnecessary. What is clear is their ability to compare messages and recognize confirmation. They use prior knowledge to accept further truth, not to defend identity. This is a model for learned readers: earlier study should make the heart more able to recognize consistent revelation, not more invested in rejecting anything that corrects previous assumptions. Knowledge serves best when it becomes a bridge to further truth, without pride and with gratitude.",
  "31": "“Respond to the caller of Allah” is the jinn warners’ direct invitation. The caller is Muhammad, who calls toward Allah rather than himself. Responding means receiving the Quran, accepting the messenger, and obeying the Lord. The phrase honors the Prophet while keeping the destination Allah.\n\n“Believe in him” makes faith in Muhammad necessary. A person cannot claim to follow Moses while rejecting the final messenger after his truth becomes clear. Prophets confirm one another. Belief includes trusting Muhammad’s revelation and following his authentic guidance.\n\n“He will forgive your sins” gives hope before warning. Jinn, like humans, can sin and repent. Scholars discuss details of whether “some” sins or all are intended by the grammar, but sincere faith and repentance are established means of forgiveness. “Protect you from a painful punishment” names rescue from final consequence.\n\nThe invitation balances command, promise, and warning. It does not offer worldly wealth or social dominance as proof. Forgiveness and protection are greater needs. The jinn had heard one recitation and understood that good news should be carried with urgency. Believers calling others should similarly center Allah, the Messenger, pardon, and safety rather than personal loyalty. The response remains voluntary, but the next verse explains that refusal cannot create escape. The jinn say “your sins,” addressing their community directly and acknowledging real moral burden. They do not blame human beings, fate, or an unseen class of devils for every wrong. Jinn can tempt one another just as humans can, but each still responds. Their call shows repentance belongs to both species. Forgiveness is not limited by an unusual past or nature. Any morally responsible creature reached by Muhammad’s message must answer, and sincere belief opens protection by the same merciful Lord. Their call is therefore always universal in message and completely personal in responsibility. That responsibility reaches every listener.",
  "32": "“Whoever does not respond to the caller of Allah” identifies refusal after the invitation. The caller presents Allah’s message, so rejection is not merely a personal disagreement with a speaker. Responsibility still depends on access and understanding, which Allah knows perfectly. The jinn warn people who can hear their report.\n\n“Will have no escape on earth” means no distance, hiding place, or speed can leave Allah’s kingdom. Jinn may travel in ways humans cannot, but they too remain created and confined by His rule. Greater mobility does not create independence.\n\n“Nor will they have any protectors against Him” removes allies and false spiritual powers. A protector might help within creation, but none can oppose the Creator’s judgment. The jinn do not present themselves as protectors to be worshipped; they direct their people to Allah.\n\n“They are clearly astray” follows from rejecting the only safe response. Clarity lies in direction, not intelligence or extraordinary ability. An unseen being with unusual powers can still be lost, while an ordinary human can be guided. The verse therefore rejects superstition about jinn as all-knowing spiritual patrons. They need revelation like humans. Safety belongs to responding to Allah’s caller, not seeking secret protection from creatures who cannot protect themselves against Him. The words “on earth” are especially striking for jinn, whose movement may make ordinary human barriers irrelevant. Even such abilities cannot cross the boundary of creation. This corrects people who fear jinn as though they rival Allah. Seeking protection through occult practices often hands the heart to creatures that cannot escape their own Lord. Authentic protection lies in Allah, prayer, Quranic remembrance, and lawful practical care. A created being may frighten, but only the Creator owns ultimate safety and judgment. Clear guidance directly frees the heart from occult fear and from lasting false dependence.",
  "33": "“Allah, Who created the heavens and the earth” returns denial of resurrection to the largest visible evidence. The One who originated such vast creation does not face difficulty restoring human bodies. Creation from nothing is, in human reasoning, a greater display than re-creation from known matter.\n\n“Did not tire in creating them” denies fatigue, weakness, or need for rest. Allah’s creative act does not resemble human labor. The Quran’s six days of creation are chosen stages of wisdom, not evidence of exhaustion. Any theology assigning divine rest from fatigue projects creaturely limitation onto Him.\n\n“Is able to give life to the dead?” is a rhetorical question answered, “Yes indeed!” Resurrection is not merely possible; Allah has promised it. “He is certainly Most Capable of everything” makes power complete.\n\nThe verse does not ask science to reconstruct resurrection through current processes. It asks reason to compare the denied act with the admitted Creator. Bodies may scatter, but every particle and identity remains known. The heavens and earth also show sustained power at every moment. A person who admits that origin while denying return has not followed the evidence to its conclusion. Creation above and around us is a continuous answer to graves below. “Did not tire” also carries comfort for people asking whether countless resurrections could burden divine power. Number makes tasks hard for creatures because attention and energy are limited. Allah’s knowledge holds every person without confusion, and His power does not diminish through use. Raising all humanity and jinn is no harder for Him than creating the first. The verse therefore answers both material decay and vast number. Neither scattering nor population creates a problem for the Lord of heavens and earth. Divine power remains complete before every grave and every generation. He never tires, and no creature is ever lost to His knowledge.",
  "34": "“The Day the disbelievers will be exposed to the Fire” repeats a scene from verse 20, now focusing on forced recognition. Repetition joins their exhausted pleasures, arrogance, and denial of resurrection to the same destination. The Fire they treated as warning becomes the evidence before them.\n\n“Is this Hereafter not the truth?” is not a request for information. It makes them testify against their earlier claim. “Absolutely, by our Lord!” admits truth and Lordship, but only after sight has removed meaningful choice. An oath now cannot replace faith when the unseen was announced.\n\n“Then taste the punishment for your disbelief” links consequence to persistent rejection, not innocent ignorance. “Taste” means direct experience, and disbelief here includes refusal after signs. Allah knows individual access and never wrongs anyone.\n\nThe verse invites an earlier answer. A living person can say the Hereafter is truth through faith and let that testimony guide repentance. Waiting until Fire is visible produces accurate words without saving trust. This also shows why mock demands for immediate punishment are foolish: the requested demonstration ends debate by ending opportunity. Revelation’s verbal question is mercy because it can be answered now with a changed life. Their words “by our Lord” also show that denial often ends in acknowledgment of the very Lord previously resisted. The issue was never that the Creator needed their testimony to become true. They needed faith for testimony to benefit them. An oath under unavoidable sight has no moral risk and therefore no role in the earlier test. Present faith accepts trustworthy evidence before compulsion. It can still ask questions, but it does not demand the Fire as the final proof before choosing to obey. Voluntary testimony now is the one that can shape the final answer. Faith must answer now, before unavoidable sight removes the opportunity to choose.",
  "35": "“Endure patiently, as did the Messengers of Firm Resolve” tells Muhammad to follow the strongest examples among prophets. Many scholars identify Noah, Abraham, Moses, Jesus, and Muhammad as the five “Ulul-Azm,” while some early explanations include all messengers marked by firm patience. The point is steadfast obedience under prolonged rejection, not a title for pride.\n\n“Do not seek to hasten the torment for the deniers” protects prophetic concern and submission. Impatience might wish judgment would end mockery, but Allah knows who may repent and when justice should come. The messenger warns without controlling the sentence.\n\n“When they see what they have been threatened with, it will be as if they had only stayed in this world for an hour of a day” shows worldly life shrinking beside eternity. Long comfort and long persecution both appear brief when final reality arrives. “This is a sufficient warning” means the message has been clearly delivered.\n\n“Will anyone be destroyed except the rebellious people?” affirms moral cause. Allah’s destruction is not random; it reaches those persisting outside His command. The verse closes the chapter with patience, proportion, and justice. For believers, one difficult lifetime is an hour moving toward mercy. For deniers, delay is an hour in which rebellion can still be left before warning becomes sight. The Messengers of Firm Resolve also differed in the forms and lengths of their trials. Noah preached for generations, Abraham faced fire and migration, Moses confronted Pharaoh, and Jesus faced rejection; Muhammad’s path combined persecution, migration, leadership, and conflict. Their shared quality was fidelity, not identical method. Patience must therefore follow revelation and circumstance. It may mean speaking, leaving, defending, forgiving, or waiting. What remains constant is refusal to abandon Allah’s command for emotional haste. Their patience was faithful, active obedience sustained until Allah chose the outcome."
};
